---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import Workspace from '../../components/Workspace.astro';
import routesJson from '../../data/routes.json';
import {titleFor,topicIntro,howTo,category,commonMistakes} from '../../lib/site';
import {sampleProblem} from '../../lib/math';
import {relatedSkillTasks} from '../../lib/intent-relations';
import {correctedTaskLinksFor} from '../../lib/seo-routing-r70';
import seoTargets from '../../data/seo-targets.json';
import {topicOptionsFor,maxQuantityForTopic} from '../../lib/topic-options';
import {hasVerifiedSteps} from '../../lib/capabilities';
export function getStaticPaths(){return routesJson.filter(x=>x.type!=='HOLD').map(r=>({params:{slug:r.slug},props:{route:r}}));}
const {route}=Astro.props;
const title=titleFor(route.slug);
const categories=routesJson.filter(x=>x.type!=='HOLD'&&x.slug!==route.slug&&category(x.slug)===category(route.slug)).slice(0,6);
const preview=`/images/worksheets/${route.slug}-sample.webp`;
const relatedSkills=relatedSkillTasks(route.slug);
const reviewedTaskRoutes=correctedTaskLinksFor(route.slug);
const fixedSample=sampleProblem(route.slug,20261104);
const seoTarget=seoTargets.find(entry=>entry.slug===route.slug);
if(!seoTarget)throw new Error('Missing source-backed SEO intent for '+route.slug);
const specialized=route.type==='CONDITIONAL_TOOL';
const focusHeading=seoTarget.primary[0].toUpperCase()+seoTarget.primary.slice(1);
const topicsWithOptions=topicOptionsFor(route.slug).map(def=>({
 label:def.label,
 choices:def.choices.slice(0,6).map(choice=>choice.label).join(', '),
 remainder:Math.max(0,def.choices.length-6)
}));
const defaultMax=maxQuantityForTopic(route.slug,{},'standard');
const stepPdf=hasVerifiedSteps(route.slug);
const hubSpecialists=relatedSkills.filter(skill=>routesJson.find(r=>r.slug===skill.slug)?.type==='CONDITIONAL_TOOL');

---
<Layout title={`${title} – Free Printable PDF & Answer Key | Math Worksheet Studio`} description={topicIntro(route.slug)} canonicalPath={`/${route.slug}/`}>
<div class="container"><section class="hero hero-compact"><div class="crumb"><a href="/">All math worksheets</a> / {category(route.slug)}</div><span class="eyebrow">Make it · Print it · Practice it</span><h1>{title}</h1><p>{topicIntro(route.slug)}</p></section><Workspace slug={route.slug}/>
{specialized?<section class="panel info-panel" data-seo-focus aria-label="Worksheet content and customization">
<span class="eyebrow">Exact practice scope</span>
<h2>{focusHeading}: printable practice options</h2>
<p>Create a fresh practice sheet for this specific math skill using the generator above. These are <strong>automatically generated questions</strong>, not scans of a fixed worksheet or promises of alignment to a particular school grade.</p>
<h3>What is included?</h3>
<ul>
<li>Easy, Standard or Challenge number ranges and up to {defaultMax} questions in Standard mode (some topic settings have lower limits).</li>
{topicsWithOptions.map(option=><li><strong>{option.label}:</strong> {option.choices}{option.remainder>0? ` and ${option.remainder} more choices`:''}.</li>)}
<li>Separate student questions and matching teacher answer keys on A4 or US Letter, both reproducible with the same seed.</li>
<li>{stepPdf?'Step-by-step solutions are also available for this verified topic.':'A step-by-step PDF is not offered for this topic; use the separate answer key.'}</li>
</ul>
</section>:<section class="panel info-panel" data-seo-hub aria-label="Broad practice collection and specialist navigation">
<span class="eyebrow">Choose the right skill</span>
<h2>{title}: general practice and focused alternatives</h2>
<p>This collection offers its supported question types, but does not automatically cover every related math skill or grade-level search. If you need a specific task, use a dedicated practice generator instead.</p>
{hubSpecialists.length>0&&<ul>{hubSpecialists.map(skill=><li><a href={`/${skill.slug}/`}>{titleFor(skill.slug)}</a> — {skill.difference}</li>)}</ul>}
</section>}
<section class="info-grid"><div class="panel info-panel"><h2>How to use this worksheet</h2><ol>{howTo(route.slug).map(item=><li>{item}</li>)}</ol><p>Refresh the seed to generate another distinct set. For review, download the separate answer PDF instead of printing answers over the questions. Worked steps are available only on step-verified topics.</p></div><div class="panel info-panel"><h2>Example printable practice</h2><p>A fixed-seed preview created from the same deterministic generator used above.</p><img class="sample-img" src={preview} width="1000" height="560" alt={`${title} printable practice sample with real generated question`}/></div></section>
{(route.slug==='one-step-equations-worksheets'||route.slug==='two-step-equations-worksheets')&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r65-equation-guide aria-label="Decimal and fraction equation practice">
<span class="eyebrow">Exact number practice</span>
<h2>{route.slug==='one-step-equations-worksheets'?'One-step decimal and fraction equations':'Two-step decimal and fraction equations'} with printable answer keys</h2>
<p>The main worksheet generator above continues to create <strong>integer-coefficient equations</strong>. For more variety, the separate “Decimal and fraction equation practice” panel adds precisely calculated decimal and fraction equation packs. They are real exercises, not merely examples or keyword landing-page text.</p>
<ol><li>Set your difficulty, number of questions (4–40), seed, equation form or coefficient sign, and US Letter or A4 paper.</li><li>Select <strong>Decimals</strong> to use finite decimal values, or <strong>Fractions</strong> for reduced rational values.</li><li>Download the student practice PDF, the matching answer key, or a worked-step PDF. Use the same settings and number type to reproduce exactly the same pack.</li></ol>
<p>{route.slug==='one-step-equations-worksheets'?'One-step packs include addition/subtraction forms and multiplication forms.':'Two-step packs solve ax + b = c with nonzero decimal or fractional coefficients and exact rational solutions.'} These extensions do not currently provide equation word problems, inequalities, or curriculum-certified grades. The original reproducible worksheet link does <strong>not</strong> encode the separate pack number-type selector.</p>
</section>}
{(route.slug==='number-line-worksheets'||route.slug==='fractions-worksheets')&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r66-fraction-guide aria-label="Fraction number line exercise instructions">
<span class="eyebrow">New exact fraction diagrams</span>
<h2>Fractions on a number line: identify and plot printable exercises</h2>
<p>The original worksheet above still produces the same integer number-line or fraction arithmetic practice as before. The <strong>separate fraction number-line panel</strong> now prints actual equally spaced vector lines for 0–1 and 0–2, with denominators 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10 or 12.</p>
<ol><li>Choose <strong>Identify the fraction</strong> to read a pre-marked dot, or <strong>Plot the given fraction</strong> to put a dot on a blank, partitioned line.</li><li>Select 0–1 for proper fractions or 0–2 for proper and improper fractions.</li><li>Use the existing seed, difficulty, questions and A4/Letter settings. Download the student practice PDF and the separate answer PDF; exact matching dots and, where useful, simplified equivalent fractions appear only in the teacher key.</li></ol>
<p>These are individual fraction-point exercises, not fraction addition or subtraction with jumps, mixed-number word problems, or automatically graded online work. The original share link does not encode these extra selectors. The existing generator v18 is unchanged.</p>
</section>}
{(route.slug==='addition-worksheets'||route.slug==='subtraction-worksheets'||route.slug==='mixed-addition-subtraction-worksheets')&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r68-decimal-guide aria-label="Decimal column addition and subtraction guidance">
<span class="eyebrow">Printable exact decimal columns</span>
<h2>Adding and subtracting decimals worksheets — line up the decimal points</h2>
<p>The original whole-number worksheet above is unchanged. The separate <strong>Decimal addition and subtraction</strong> panel now creates genuine vertical column problems with tenths or hundredths, matching student worksheets and teacher answer PDFs. The decimal points align exactly on the printed page; answers use exact fixed-point calculations instead of floating-point rounding.</p>
<ol><li>Choose <strong>addition</strong>, <strong>subtraction</strong> or a <strong>mixed</strong> worksheet, then tenths, hundredths or both.</li><li>Select regrouping: mixed, carry/borrow required, or no carry/borrow. Subtraction uses nonnegative results, and each operand is displayed to the same selected decimal precision.</li><li>Use the existing seed, difficulty, question count, Letter/A4 paper and extra writing-room choice. Download the student column worksheet, matching teacher key or inverse-operation worked steps.</li></ol>
<p>Example: <strong>12.50 − 3.75 = 8.75</strong>. On the PDF, the two operands and their answer share one vertical decimal-point column. This feature does not generate money word problems, negative differences, thousandths, or curricula certified by an educational authority. The original Copy reproducible link does not encode these additional decimal settings.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='fractions-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r72-fraction-strips-guide aria-label="Fraction strips printable visual model">
<span class="eyebrow">True printable visual fractions</span>
<h2>Fraction strips printable: blank coloring model and labelled reference</h2>
<p>A fraction strip represents one whole divided into equal pieces. The separate <strong>Printable fraction strips</strong> panel produces real vector strips, not screenshots or text-only fractions. Every row has exactly the same physical width, making it possible to compare halves, thirds, quarters, fifths, sixths, eighths, tenths and twelfths as parts of the same whole.</p>
<ol><li>Choose the basic denominator set (1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8) or extended set (adds 5, 10 and 12).</li><li>Print the <strong>blank coloring strips</strong> to shade or cut individual unit fractions. Print the <strong>labelled teacher reference</strong> for the exact 1/n identity inside every partition.</li><li>Select US Letter or A4 in the worksheet settings. Because this is a fixed one-page reference chart, worksheet seed, question quantity and difficulty are not relevant to strip partition geometry.</li></ol>
<p>Each complete strip is 420 PDF points wide and is divided into equally sized vector cells. Print at <strong>Actual size (100%)</strong>. This tool covers unit-fraction strips only; other picture-based fractions, circles and percentage visual models remain separate, unclaimed skills.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='fractions-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r69-conversion-guide aria-label="Fractions and decimals exact conversion worksheet guide">
<span class="eyebrow">Exact two-way conversion practice</span>
<h2>Fractions to decimals worksheets and decimals to simplified fractions</h2>
<p>The original fraction arithmetic generator above is unchanged. A separate printable conversion panel now creates real exercises: convert fractions to terminating decimals, turn decimals into fractions in lowest terms, or alternate both tasks on one worksheet.</p>
<ol><li>Choose <strong>fraction → decimal</strong>, <strong>decimal → simplest fraction</strong> or a <strong>mixed worksheet</strong>. The mix alternates both conversion directions.</li><li>Choose exact tenths, hundredths, thousandths or a mix. Answers are mathematically exact, not rounded or repeating decimal approximations.</li><li>Keep the same seed, difficulty, number of questions, US Letter/A4 paper and writing-room settings to download reproducible student, answer and worked-step PDFs. The fraction is drawn with a real fraction bar.</li></ol>
<p>Examples: <strong>3/8 = 0.375</strong>; <strong>0.75 = 3/4</strong> in simplest form. Improper fractions may appear. This exercise does not claim fraction strips, negative-number conversion, percentages, recurring decimals, or certification for a particular grade. The original Copy reproducible link does not include these additional selectors.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='division-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r73-division-stories-guide aria-label="Division word problems with interpreted remainders">
<span class="eyebrow">More than just arithmetic</span>
<h2>Division word problems: equal sharing, leftovers and rounding up</h2>
<p>The original division facts generator above remains unchanged. The separate <strong>Division word problems</strong> panel creates actual everyday situations with one-digit divisors and two- or three-digit totals. These are text questions, not number facts with added filler. The story determines whether the answer is a per-person share, a number of full boxes and leftovers, extra vehicles needed, or just the remainder.</p>
<ol><li>Choose <strong>Mixed stories</strong> for all four contexts, or select exactly one skill.</li><li>Keep your seed, difficulty, question count, US Letter/A4 paper and writing-room options. Download separate student practice, matching answer key or worked solutions.</li><li>For every answer, check that divisor × whole quotient + remainder equals the total. Then state the real-world units and explain why the last incomplete group is or is not counted.</li></ol>
<p>Example: 30 students sharing minibuses of 8 seats need <strong>4 minibuses</strong>, not 3, because the remaining 6 students still need a ride. In contrast, 30 stickers used 8 per complete card make <strong>3 full cards with 6 stickers left over</strong>. These are one-step whole-number word problems, not multistep algebra or certified grade-specific assessments. The original Copy reproducible link does not include these extra story selectors.</p>
</section>}
{(route.slug==='multiplication-worksheets'||route.slug==='division-worksheets')&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r71-multdiv-guide aria-label="Mixed multiplication division facts, fact families and rectangular area models">
<span class="eyebrow">New actual math practice modes</span>
<h2>Multiplication and division fact families{route.slug==='multiplication-worksheets'?' and area-model multiplication':''}</h2>
<p>The normal worksheet above still creates the original integer arithmetic facts. The separate <strong>Multiplication &amp; division skill packs</strong> panel now generates real, differently structured printable exercises.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Mixed multiplication and division:</strong> alternate products and exact quotient facts with no remainders, then check answers with the matching teacher PDF.</li><li><strong>Fact families:</strong> given two distinct factors and their product, complete four linked equations (two multiplications and their two inverse divisions). The teacher key prints all four correct identities.</li>{route.slug==='multiplication-worksheets'&&<li><strong>Area-model multiplication:</strong> split a two-digit factor into tens and ones, multiply each partition by a one- or two-digit factor, then add the partial areas. Student diagrams have blank cells; the answer PDF shows exact partial products. Diagrams are schematic, not to scale.</li>}</ol>
<p>Choose the original seed, difficulty, question count and A4 or Letter paper, then download separate student, answer and worked-step PDFs. The original Copy reproducible link applies only to the original v18 worksheet; it does not encode these extra practice modes. These packs do not claim pictorial dot arrays, lattice multiplication or mathematical story problems.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='division-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r67-decimal-guide aria-label="Decimal division worksheet guide">
<span class="eyebrow">New printable decimal division practice</span>
<h2>Decimal division worksheets with exact answers and worked solutions</h2>
<p>The original division worksheet generator above continues to create <strong>whole-number division facts</strong>. The separate decimal division panel now prints genuine decimal questions using an exact noninteger quotient and either a whole-number divisor or a decimal divisor. It is not a replacement for the original v18 generator.</p>
<ol><li>Select the difficulty, number of questions, seed, US Letter or A4, and optional extra pencil room.</li><li>Under <strong>Decimal division practice</strong>, choose whole divisors, decimal divisors or a mix. Choose tenths, hundredths or mixed exact quotients.</li><li>Download student questions, the matching answer-key PDF, or a PDF with equivalent whole-number division and multiplication checks. Use the same seed and selectors to reproduce all three.</li></ol>
<p>These packs use terminating answers only: there are no remainder questions, recurring decimals or rounding instructions. The original Copy reproducible link encodes only the original integer worksheet, not the new decimal settings. The existing long-division page is a different task.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='multiplication-tables-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" aria-label="Blank times table and answer chart" data-r64-chart-guide>
<span class="eyebrow">New printable chart</span><h2>Blank multiplication table 1–12 and complete answer PDF</h2>
<p>A multiplication table is not the same as a shuffled set of fact questions. This page now offers both: the normal customizable facts generator above, plus a separate <strong>12 × 12 multiplication chart</strong> with row and column headings 1–12 and 144 blank answer boxes.</p>
<ol><li>Select US Letter or A4 under Customize worksheet.</li><li>Choose <strong>Blank 1–12 chart PDF</strong> for students to fill in products; the starting row and column numbers stay visible.</li><li>Choose <strong>Completed chart answer PDF</strong> when checking every product; it lists exactly the matching 144 answers.</li></ol>
<p>The chart is one page, generated locally as a real vector PDF, and is independent of the randomized 4–40-question facts worksheets. It is not a timed test, a 0–12 chart or a grid restricted to products less than 100. Print at Actual size for consistent handwriting space.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='area-of-trapezoids-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" aria-label="Trapezoid definition choices"><h2>Which trapezoid definition does your classroom use?</h2><p>Some curricula define a trapezoid as a quadrilateral with <strong>at least one pair of parallel sides</strong>, allowing equal parallel bases. Others require <strong>exactly one pair</strong> of parallel sides; in those curricula, a figure with equal bases is not treated as a trapezoid.</p><p>In <strong>Customize worksheet → Parallel-base definition</strong>, select “Unequal parallel bases only” if your class excludes these figures. The default inclusive option preserves earlier reproducible worksheet seeds. All drawings are schematic, not to scale.</p></section>}
{route.slug==='inequalities-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r74-inequality-guide aria-label="Translate words into inequalities and graph compound inequalities">
<span class="eyebrow">R74 exact language and interval skills</span>
<h2>Translating sentences into inequalities and compound AND/OR worksheets</h2>
<p>Words determine the comparison symbol: <strong>at most</strong> means ≤, <strong>at least</strong> means ≥, <strong>less than</strong> means &lt;, and <strong>greater than</strong> means &gt;. Our separate printable pack asks students to translate phrases involving addition, subtraction, positive-number multiplication and division into inequalities, rather than simply copy the expression.</p>
<ol><li>Choose <strong>Translate English</strong> to practice the strict and inclusive comparison vocabulary. Example: “x increased by 4 is at most 10” becomes <strong>x + 4 ≤ 10</strong>, so x ≤ 6.</li><li>Choose <strong>Compound AND</strong> to express an overlap such as <strong>−2 ≤ x &lt; 5</strong>. Its number-line graph fills the −2 endpoint, leaves 5 open, and shades between.</li><li>Choose <strong>Compound OR</strong> to express separate rays such as <strong>x &lt; −2 or x ≥ 5</strong>, with shading outside the gap. The teacher PDF uses hollow or filled dots to represent strict/non-strict endpoints.</li></ol>
<p>Select your seed, difficulty, question count, Letter/A4 paper and writing-room choice before downloading separate student, key and worked-solution PDFs. The original one/two-step inequalities generator and share URL remain unchanged. These exercises do not include triangle-inequality geometry or systems of inequalities.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='area-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r74-circle-guide aria-label="Circle area and sector area formula">
<span class="eyebrow">R74 exact circle geometry</span>
<h2>Circle area worksheets: radius, diameter and sector area</h2>
<p>Area of a complete circle is <strong>A = πr²</strong>; radius is half the diameter. For a sector with central angle θ in degrees, use <strong>A = (θ / 360) × πr²</strong>. This page now offers an opt-in sector and circle-area generator in addition to the existing rectangle/square worksheets.</p>
<ol><li>Choose a full circle with a given radius or diameter; compute the positive radius before squaring it.</li><li>Choose a semicircle (180°), a quarter-circle (90°), or a minor sector with angles from 30° through 180°. The diagrams draw the stated angle from the circle center.</li><li>Choose <strong>Find radius from area</strong> to reverse A = πr² using the positive square root. Print matching student questions, teacher key and worked solutions in US Letter or A4.</li></ol>
<p>Example: a quarter-circle of radius 6 cm has area <strong>9π cm²</strong>. An angle of 60° and radius 3 cm gives <strong>(3/2)π cm²</strong>. Answers use exact reduced rational multiples of π; no 3.14 approximation is assumed. This does not claim circumference, arc length, composite shaded areas or sphere volume. The original seeded worksheet and share link are unaffected.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='inequalities-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r75-triangle-guide aria-label="Exact triangle inequality theorem and integer side ranges">
<span class="eyebrow">R75 geometry, not symbolic algebra</span>
<h2>Triangle inequality theorem worksheets — which side lengths form a real triangle?</h2>
<p>For three positive side lengths a, b and c to form a <strong>nondegenerate</strong> triangle, every side must be less than the sum of the other two. For a missing third side x, the equivalent rule is <strong>|a − b| &lt; x &lt; a + b</strong>. Both endpoints are strictly excluded, because equality produces a straight line rather than a triangle.</p>
<ol><li>Check proposed side sets and state whether they form a real triangle: sides 2, 3 and 5 cannot make one, because 2 + 3 = 5.</li><li>Find the complete strict interval for x when two side lengths are given; for 5 and 8, the answer is <strong>3 &lt; x &lt; 13</strong>.</li><li>Count all permitted positive whole-number third sides. For 5 and 8, x can be 4 through 12, giving <strong>9 integer lengths</strong>.</li></ol>
<p>Use the Triangle theorem panel above for matching student worksheets, answer keys and worked steps. The diagrams show individual segments to scale; they never disguise an impossible side set as an ordinary triangle. This exercise does not address triangle side-angle ordering or prove every triangle theorem. Existing algebra-inequality share URLs do not encode these extra modes.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='area-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r75-composite-guide aria-label="Exact area for L-shaped two dimensional composite polygons">
<span class="eyebrow">R75 compound area geometry</span>
<h2>Composite figures area worksheets — subtract a cutout or add two rectangles</h2>
<p>A genuine L-shaped region is formed by removing a smaller rectangle from the upper-right corner of a larger rectangle. The formula is <strong>A = W × H − w × h</strong>, where W and H are the outer dimensions and w and h are the cutout's width and height. You may also split the L into nonoverlapping rectangles and add their areas.</p>
<ol><li>For an outer rectangle 10 cm wide and 8 cm high, with a 3 cm × 2 cm top-right cutout, the result is <strong>10 × 8 − 3 × 2 = 74 cm²</strong>.</li><li>Alternatively, split into a left strip of 7 × 8 and a lower-right strip of 3 × 6: <strong>56 + 18 = 74 cm²</strong>.</li><li>Read the four labelled lengths on the real vector L-shaped polygon before computing, then include squared units in the answer.</li></ol>
<p>Select subtraction, partitioned addition or mixed practice in the separate Composite area panel. All generated student, teacher and worked-step PDFs share their settings and are independent of the original rectangle/square worksheets. Only axis-aligned L-shaped figures with one rectangular notch are covered, not arbitrary shaded or curved composites.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='surface-area-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r76-cylinder-guide aria-label="Cylinder net surface area and volume exact pi formulas">
<span class="eyebrow">R76 printable cylinder geometry</span>
<h2>Cylinder surface area worksheets — circular bases, curved surface, and volume</h2>
<p>A closed right circular cylinder has <strong>two circular ends and one curved lateral surface</strong>. With radius <strong>r</strong> and vertical height <strong>h</strong>, the curved part unwraps to a rectangle of width <strong>2πr</strong> and height <strong>h</strong>. The total surface area is <strong>2πr² + 2πrh</strong>; the lateral area alone is <strong>2πrh</strong>, and the volume is <strong>πr²h</strong>.</p>
<ol><li>For a radius of 3 cm and height of 5 cm, the circular bases total 18π cm², while the lateral rectangle contributes 30π cm².</li><li>The complete closed surface area is therefore <strong>48π cm²</strong>. If only the curved side is requested, do <strong>not</strong> include the bases.</li><li>Volume uses cubic units: <strong>π × 3² × 5 = 45π cm³</strong>. Square units for surface area and cubic units for volume are not interchangeable.</li></ol>
<p>Use the separate Cylinder panel above for reproducible student problems, matching answer keys, and worked solutions in Letter or A4 format. The diagrams are dimension-proportional unfolded nets with two circular disks and one exact circumference-by-height rectangle, rather than inaccurate perspective pictures. The original cube and rectangular-prism worksheets still work separately.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='mean-median-mode-range-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r77-frequency-dotplot-guide aria-label="Statistics dot plot frequency mode median range and how to draw">
<span class="eyebrow">R77 frequency dot plot extension</span>
<h2>Statistical dot plots — turn repeated observations into stacked frequencies</h2>
<p>A statistical dot plot places <strong>one dot for each observation</strong> above that observation's value on a horizontal numerical axis. It is not the same as a picture-counting worksheet: two dots above 4 mean the dataset includes the value 4 twice. An equal-width scale is essential, and the height of each stack gives the frequency.</p>
<ol><li>For the data <strong>[2, 3, 3, 5, 5, 5]</strong>, put one dot above 2, two dots above 3 and three above 5. No dots belong above values absent from the dataset.</li><li>The mode is <strong>5</strong> because its stack is tallest. The total number of observations is <strong>6</strong>, not three (the number of distinct values).</li><li>To read the median, expand the stacks in value order: 2, 3, 3, 5, 5, 5. The two central observations are 3 and 5, so the median is <strong>4</strong>. The range is <strong>5 − 2 = 3</strong>.</li></ol>
<p>Choose either plotting from real source data or reading frequency, mode, median and range in the dedicated Statistical dot plots panel. Student drawing pages have a blank, evenly labelled axis; reading pages reveal source dots but not the requested answer. Teacher keys and worked steps reproduce the same original data. The separate dot-counting number-recognition worksheets have a different purpose, and no new search-keyword verification is implied.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='volume-of-rectangular-prisms-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r78-prism-volume-guide aria-label="Volume of a right triangular prism and exact base-area multiplication">
<span class="eyebrow">R78 new geometry: right triangular prism</span>
<h2>Volume of a triangular prism — perpendicular triangle base and prism length</h2>
<p>A right triangular prism has two congruent triangular bases joined by three rectangular lateral faces. For a <strong>right-triangle</strong> base whose perpendicular legs measure a and b, the base area is <strong>B = ab/2</strong>. Multiply by the distance L between the triangular bases: <strong>V = B × L = abL/2</strong>.</p>
<ol><li>If the base legs are 3 cm and 4 cm, its hypotenuse is 5 cm and each triangular base has area <strong>6 cm²</strong>.</li><li>If the prism length is 7 cm, its volume is <strong>6 × 7 = 42 cm³</strong>. Volume uses cubic units, never squared units.</li><li>In the unfolded net the side rectangles measure <strong>3 × 7, 4 × 7, and 5 × 7</strong>. Their widths follow the actual triangle edge lengths; the net can also be used to understand surface area without confusing it with volume.</li></ol>
<p>Select the dedicated right triangular prism panel above for seed-reproducible student PDFs, matching answers and three-step worked solutions. The earlier rectangular-prism worksheets and share links remain unchanged. Oblique prisms and non-right triangle bases are not yet supported by this specialized generator.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='surface-area-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r78-solids-guide aria-label="Prism and pyramid surface area true nets with proper base and slant height geometry">
<span class="eyebrow">R78 true prism and pyramid nets</span>
<h2>Surface area of triangular prisms and pyramids — exact unfolded faces</h2>
<p><strong>Closed right triangular prism:</strong> Add both triangular bases and all three rectangular sides. For a right-triangle base with legs a, b, hypotenuse c and prism length L, the total surface is <strong>ab + (a + b + c)L</strong>. The printer-friendly net has these actual side widths, not arbitrary decorative proportions.</p>
<p><strong>Right square pyramid:</strong> One square base plus four isosceles triangular side faces give <strong>s² + 2sl</strong>, where <em>l is the slant height along a triangular face</em>. Do not replace l with the perpendicular distance from the apex to the base plane.</p>
<p><strong>Regular triangular pyramid (tetrahedron):</strong> This is a special triangular pyramid with four congruent equilateral faces. Each equilateral face has area √3s²/4, so its total surface is <strong>s²√3</strong>. A pyramid with unequal triangular faces does not have this shortcut formula.</p>
<ol><li>For a right triangular prism with base 3–4–5 cm and length 7 cm, surface area = 3 × 4 + (3 + 4 + 5) × 7 = <strong>96 cm²</strong>.</li><li>For a right square pyramid with base edge 8 cm and face slant height 7 cm, surface area = 64 + 2 × 8 × 7 = <strong>176 cm²</strong>.</li><li>For a regular tetrahedron whose edge is 6 cm, the four equilateral faces have combined exact area <strong>36√3 cm²</strong>.</li></ol>
<p>The dedicated surface-area panel offers a mixed set or separate solid modes. Its vector nets show two triangles plus three rectangles, one square plus four triangles, or four identical equilateral triangles. They include matching student, answer and worked-step editions. General triangular pyramids, oblique prisms, and compound three-dimensional solids are not claimed as supported.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='volume-of-rectangular-prisms-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r79-mixed-volume-guide aria-label="One mixed worksheet with rectangular prism triangular prism and cylinder volume problems">
<span class="eyebrow">R79 actual mixed-volume worksheet</span>
<h2>Volume of prisms and cylinders — three solids, one printable worksheet</h2>
<p>Practise applying the shared rule <strong>V = base area × perpendicular height</strong> across three different solids without changing worksheets. A rectangular prism has a rectangular base, a right triangular prism has a triangular base, and a right circular cylinder has a circular base.</p>
<ol><li>Rectangular prism 2 cm × 3 cm × 5 cm: <strong>V = 30 cm³</strong>.</li><li>Right triangular prism with base legs 3 cm and 4 cm, length 5 cm: <strong>V = (3 × 4 / 2) × 5 = 30 cm³</strong>.</li><li>Right circular cylinder with radius 2 cm and height 5 cm: <strong>V = π × 2² × 5 = 20π cm³</strong>.</li></ol>
<p>The Mixed prisms &amp; cylinders panel produces one document with all three types interleaved, a separate matching answer key and optional worked steps. Use at least three problems to show all three solids in mixed mode. Each PDF contains genuine proportional nets with dimensions, not arbitrary generic shapes. The older rectangular-prism and individual triangular-prism exercises remain available.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='surface-area-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r79-mixed-surface-guide aria-label="One closed surface area worksheet mixing real rectangular prisms triangular prisms and cylinders">
<span class="eyebrow">R79 actual mixed-surface worksheet</span>
<h2>Surface area of prisms and cylinders — all closed faces in one practice set</h2>
<p>For <strong>total surface area</strong>, include both bases and every lateral side. A rectangular prism has six rectangular faces; a right triangular prism has two triangular ends and three lateral rectangles; a closed right circular cylinder has two circular disks and one curved side.</p>
<ol><li>Rectangular prism 2 cm × 3 cm × 5 cm: <strong>SA = 2(6 + 10 + 15) = 62 cm²</strong>.</li><li>Right triangular prism with legs 3 cm and 4 cm, hypotenuse 5 cm and length 5 cm: <strong>SA = 3 × 4 + (3 + 4 + 5) × 5 = 72 cm²</strong>.</li><li>Closed cylinder with radius 2 cm and height 5 cm: <strong>SA = 2π × 2² + 2π × 2 × 5 = 28π cm²</strong>.</li></ol>
<p>A real rectangular-prism net displays six faces, the triangular prism net has two right-triangle bases plus three rectangles, and the cylinder net has two circular disks plus a circumference-by-height rectangle. The mixed worksheet interleaves them without replacing the original cube, cylinder or pyramid tools. Open or hollow cylinders are not part of this total-surface mode.</p>
</section>}
{route.slug==='surface-area-worksheets'&&<section class="panel info-panel" data-r80-net-surface-lesson aria-label="Learn surface area from connected solid nets and subtract joined internal faces on composite solids">
<span class="eyebrow">R80 actual net and compound surface-area exercises</span>
<h2>Surface area from nets — count every exposed face, not just the outline</h2>
<p>An unfolded solid net allows you to calculate the total surface area by summing each real two-dimensional face. The net area does not change when the solid folds. The new print-ready exercises include a rectangular prism with six rectangles, a right triangular prism with two triangles and three rectangles, a closed cylinder with two circles and a circumference-by-height rectangle, a right square pyramid with four triangular faces and a square base, and a regular tetrahedron with four congruent equilateral faces.</p>
<ol><li>For a closed rectangular prism 2 cm × 3 cm × 5 cm, the net area is <strong>2(2×3 + 2×5 + 3×5) = 62 cm²</strong>.</li><li>For a closed cylinder of radius 2 cm and height 5 cm, the net area is <strong>2π×2² + 2π×2×5 = 28π cm²</strong>. The curved lateral wall becomes a rectangle whose width is the true circumference.</li><li>For a right square pyramid, each triangular face uses the <strong>face slant height</strong>, not the vertical apex-to-base height. For a regular tetrahedron, all four triangular faces are equilateral; its exact total area is s²√3.</li></ol>
<h3>Surface area of composite solids — remove both concealed faces</h3>
<p>Joining two closed rectangular prisms creates a composite solid. The shared contact rectangle is counted once on each separate box, but <strong>neither side of this internal contact is part of the exposed exterior surface</strong>. Therefore use <strong>SA(composite) = SA(box A) + SA(box B) − 2×area(shared joint)</strong>.</p>
<p>For example, a bottom prism of 8 × 3 × 2 cm with a 4 × 3 × 2 cm top prism joined at one end has separate surface areas 92 cm² and 52 cm². The common contact face is 4 × 3 = 12 cm², so the exposed total is <strong>92 + 52 − 2×12 = 120 cm²</strong>. The true unfolded composite net comprises <strong>two congruent L-shaped end faces and six rectangles</strong> whose widths match the actual boundary lengths.</p>
<p>Use the dedicated panels for student questions, independent answer keys and fully worked steps. The composite generator is specifically limited to two full-depth, orthogonally joined rectangular prisms; it is not a general arbitrary-solid calculator. Source keyword volumes and difficulty scores remain historical snapshots, not current SEO rankings. The preview website remains excluded from indexing until a separate launch review.</p>
</section>}
